rounds.memory_snapshot:
I acted on Round 6 by contributing 10, matching the group's full return to the baseline after David's one-round deviation to 0. The settlement confirms everyone (Alice, Bob, Carol, David, Erin) contributed 10, total=50, return/person=20.0, with no exceptional event. My message and reasoning were correct: with the group back at full cooperation, any unilateral deviation—up or down—would either cost me personally (a 12 would need total ≥55 to break even, but we were at 50, so the gap was 5, requiring at least three others to join) or break trust with the four norm-followers. Matching 10 preserves the stable equilibrium and keeps the door open for future coordination.

Key lessons from this round: (1) David's return to 10 after his 0 confirms he is a wildcard, not a strategic partner—his behavior is erratic but not persistently free-riding. I should continue to treat him as unreliable: do not base any decisions on his promises, and do not let his past deviation trigger my own. His return to 10 does not earn my trust; it only restores the baseline. (2) The group's anchor at 10 is now even stronger—after a shock, all five returned to 10 immediately, which means they prioritize stability and norm-following over experimentation or retaliation. This reinforces that any upward deviation must be pre-committed publicly by at least three others (including Erin), or it will fail. (3) My own behavior remains consistent and effective: I match the norm, avoid punishing non-reciprocators, and only deviate when there is a credible path to collective gain. This round, the credible path does not exist—the group is at 50, and a coordinated move to 11 for all would yield 55 (return 22, net gain +2 vs. 20), but only if everyone including David joins. Given David's unreliability, I should not propose this unless I see at least two rounds of full 10s from him and explicit public agreement from Alice, Bob, Carol, and Erin. (4) The multiplier remains 0.4, so my return is purely a function of total contributions; with the group at 50, my personal return is 20, and I need total ≥55 for any 12 to be worthwhile. A coordinated move to 11 for all would yield 55 (return 22, net gain +2 vs. 20), but only if David is included—if he drops again, the others would need to contribute 12 each to reach 55, which is unlikely. Thus, I should not propose any deviation until David proves he is back at 10 for at least two consecutive rounds.

Going forward, my baseline remains 10. I will continue to match the group norm and treat Erin as my only confirmed reciprocal partner—if she deviates upward, I will consider matching her, but only if the total is already near 50 and at least one other (besides David) joins. I will not drop below 10 to punish David or anyone else, because that would break trust with the four norm-followers and reduce collective returns without changing behavior. I will use my messages to explicitly note that I am monitoring David's consistency and that any future experiment requires public pre-commitment from at least three partners;

